Managed Code
  - Code whose execution is managed by runtime - CLR - Common Language Runtime  
  - CLR - Take Managed code -> Compile it into machine code (IL)  -> Execute it (JIT)
  - Code runs under the control of CLR
  - CLR handles
    - Memory allocation
    - Garbage Collection
    - Exception Handling
    - Type Safety
    - Security
    - Thread Management
      string str = "Rishabh";
      - Memory is allocated on heap
      - GC cleans it automatically
      - CLR ensures safety

Unmanaged Code
  - C/C++ code
  - Programmer is in-charge of everything
  - Code that runs outside CLR control
    - Examples
      - Native C/C++
      - Windows API
      - Old COM libraries
      - OS-Level libraries
  - Manually manage the memory
  - No GC
  - No CLR safety gurantees

CLR allows passing boundaries between managed and unmanaged world. like .NET can call Native C/C++ code or C/C++ can call .NET
C# allows you to use unmanaged constructs such as pointers using unsafe context. Normally C# does not allow pointer usage like C++
But if we write
```csharp
unsafe
{
  int x = 10;
  int* ptr = &x;
```
Above we are using raw memory address. CLR safety checks are bypassed and you must enable unsafe compilation.

When managed code calls unmanaged code using interop, execution crosses the CLR boundary.
At that point, memory management and execution control are handled by unmanaged runtime, 
meaning GC does not track allocations there. 
This introduces risks like memory leaks and access violations, so such calls must be carefully handled.

If you allocate unmanaged memory:
IntPtr ptr = Marshal.AllocHGlobal(100);
We should manually free it like below, else memory leak
Marshal.FreeHGlobal(ptr);
